WEBVTT

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.090
Killer whale, the most fearsome and artful hunters in the sea.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.200
They dominate the ocean just as man dominates the land.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.130
And when man meets killer whale on the frontier between land and sea,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.800
they seem to be almost...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.640
interested in us as we are in them. Their high intelligence, of which this curiosity is but one

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:51.130
sign, has led them to colonize every ocean on earth. Like wolves, they hunt in packs, and they

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.510
have developed an extraordinary range of hunting techniques that enable them to catch whatever prey

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.230
they find.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.800
is safe from killer whales not the smallest herring nor the largest seal they are the wolves of the sea

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.420
along the Pacific coast of Northwest America. From Alaska south to British Columbia, the ocean

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:57.520
is bordered by high mountain ranges. Glaciers flowing down the flanks of these great mountains

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.600
have carved out huge valleys.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.360
At the end of the last ice age, when the level of the seas rose,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.970
the lower parts of these valleys were flooded and became long, deep fields.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.600
Off the coast in early summer, salmon gather,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.840
before starting their annual journey up the rivers to spawn.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.020
And as they arrive, so do the killers.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.000
These particular killer whales specialise in hunting salmon,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.440
and they know exactly where and when to go in order to intercept the shoals.

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.080
The salmon are making their way up the flooded valleys

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.800
towards the particular rivers in which they were born.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.230
Sea lions are already there, waiting for them.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.980
But the salmon's greatest enemies are humans.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.180
and killer whales, and both are on their way in pursuit of them.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:48.760
They are travelling up Johnston Strait

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.860
between Vancouver Island and the mainland of British Columbia.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.650
The strait is a major pathway for the salmon.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:12.170
As the fish swim along it, they run straight into the necks that the fishermen have set in their path.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:22.990
The haul is so rich that there's plenty for everyone.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:28.760
And these days, the fishermen don't shoot the whales as they once did, but allow them to take a share.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.160
Sixteen groups of whales come each summer to the strait.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:41.660
Each group or pod, often about 20 strong, is an extended family,

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:47.490
usually containing three generations, a grandmother, her sons and daughters, and her daughter's offspring.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:57.170
The whales have good eyesight, but often the water is so murky they need another way of navigating and finding their prey.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.980
And they use sonar, emitting a...

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:16.540
stream of clicking sounds and listening to the echoes. A pod swims along Johnston Strait in a

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:21.840
spread out formation, each whale listening to the echoes of its own clicks and those produced by

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:29.820
its companions. Salmon, travelling in shoals, have a characteristic swimming pattern.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.910
which produces a particular kind of sonar reflection.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.440
Once the whales have found the shoal,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.340
they don't herd the fish as a group,

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.040
but chase them individually.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.500
is in on a fish, it often switches off its sonar and uses its eyes to make the catch.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:28.560
A group of researchers here in British Columbia has been studying these whales for over 20

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:28.940
years.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:32.470
John Ford is one of the founder members.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.140
I'm fascinated by the animals for many different reasons.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.760
There's so much mystery to them.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.330
There's so many questions that we are striving to answer

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.130
that it's enough to keep you motivated for years.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.680
John's special interest is the killer's use of sound.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.180
He's found out that, as well as the clicks for locating fish,

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.890
they make other calls to communicate with each other.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:05.320
In a way, it is their own kind of unique language,

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.380
but it's not a language like humans have.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:12.240
We shouldn't expect them to have nouns and verbs like we do

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.880
because it's how they make the calls

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.700
that seems to convey the really important information

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.200
that the animals need in order to lead their lives.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.980
By using an underwater microphone, John has discussed...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.320
that each family pod has its own unique vocabulary,

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:35.180
its own dialect, as it were.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.510
He and his colleagues have named each pod with a letter of the alphabet,

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:42.510
and he can immediately recognize which group is calling.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.940
H is the only pod that makes that form of call.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:58.750
There it is again, it's a really strong descending tone that is classically H.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.140
and that's another very distinctive H call.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.960
He can also identify and sex each adult in a pod

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.660
by the markings on its fin

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.560
and the shape of the saddle-like patch on its back,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.860
and each has been given a number.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:23.410
Oh, it's A6 and it looks like A38.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.560
Often they come under and investigate the hydrophone underwater.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.720
It's like A39.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.850
Those with very tall fins are mature males.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:44.880
Killers are the largest of the dolphins, up to 30 feet long and weighing as much as 7 tons.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:53.400
They can live for over 40 years, and the females produce their first young at about 15.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:59.980
When a calf is born into a pod of killer whales, it grows up with the sounds that...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.740
have developed over generations in that group. They become fixed into their

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:10.310
memory and those are the calls that that individual will make for its life.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.360
This year, I-15 Pod has a calf with them that John hasn't seen before.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:24.910
There's the little guy rolling around with his mother. You can see it's really young,

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.780
probably only a few weeks or maybe a month old because of the very patchy, mottily...

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:34.670
gray coloration on its back. It's really typical for the young calves to shed a lot of their skin

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:41.450
not long after birth. They're all having a look around, it seems. Here's the little bit.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:49.650
These killers have an extraordinary social system. The calves, even the males, stay in the same pod

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:55.060
with their mothers throughout their lives. That raises the question of how they avoid inbreeding.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:07.700
The answer may lie with the social gatherings that form in Johnston Strait in late summer,

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:11.780
when the salmon season is almost over and the whales are well fed.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.100
They seem to be in a boisterous mood.

00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:19.360
There's a great deal of excited calling,

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.780
and they're likely to play with any object they come across, even a jellyfish.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.330
Individuals from different families rub shoulders, literally.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.530
Now would be the time when a male could mate with a female from another pod.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:59.480
But unexpectedly, many of the sexually active groups that form during these big assemblies

00:10:59.480 --> 00:10:59.980
consider...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.090
entirely of males. This is one swimming upside down in the mating position.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:14.200
One of the more peculiar behaviors of killer whales is the sexual play that goes on among males in

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:21.320
pods and males from different pods when pods are traveling together. This may go on for hours at a

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:28.400
time with males rolling over each other, usually with erections, and sometimes it gets fairly

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.740
physical with animals.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:41.110
out of the water and rolling and tussling with each other. We don't really know what

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:47.350
the function of the interactions are, but it seems to be where the big mature bulls through

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:54.370
various sexual displays or physical displays demonstrate that they are dominant over others

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:58.460
that might be competing with them for access to breeding females.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:50.850
After a hectic bout of socialising like this, some of the whales will often head for a particular rocky beach on Vancouver Island.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:59.640
Tidal currents have here created a bed of uniformly sized pebbles, and this seems to have an irresistible attraction to the whales.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:05.030
Apparently, rubbing themselves along it gives them a most delectable sensation.

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.540
Even when the salmon season is at its height,

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:23.650
they will break off from foraging as often as four or five times a day

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:25.650
to come here and rub themselves.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.420
Many whale researchers first observed this behaviour in 1973,

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:37.900
but local fishermen say that whales were here doing this long before that.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:54.820
The whales reduced their buoyancy by expelling air from their blowholes

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:58.250
so that streams of bubbles are continually blistering the water.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:06.630
The whales have probably been indulging in these massages generation after generation.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:14.000
This practice, like their hunting techniques, must be counted as one of the cultural traditionals of the Johnston Strait killers.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:29.970
While this is going on, very remarkable, their cause...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:37.270
change, as John Ford has discovered. They make all sorts of strange squeaks and

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:41.230
squawks, kinds of sounds that you never hear repeated in the same form, and

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:47.470
occasionally out will pop a call, usually rather a poor imitation of a call, that

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:48.550
belongs in a different dialect.

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.490
By the end of summer, the party is over in Johnston Strait.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.340
The salmon runs have finished

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:11.690
and the killer whales spread out along the coast of British Columbia.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.110
Fattened by the bounty of summer,

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:16.050
they're now ready to cope with winter

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:18.120
when fish will be more difficult to find.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.060
But there are other flushes of plenty

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.960
in the annual cycle of this coast.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:38.790
In the early spring, when the phase of the moon and the water temperature are just right,

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:47.600
schools of herring migrate inshore to spawn, come in millions,

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:52.200
and are met by schools of hungry dog sharks.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:17.040
Rhinoceros orklips, the northern hemisphere's equivalent of penguins, join in the feast.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:39.000
The killer whales in British Columbia seldom bother with these smaller fish.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.340
But on the other side of the world, on the west coast of Norway,

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:44.660
other killer whales have a different tradition,

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:47.930
and they rely on herring as a major part of their diet.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:59.960
Here in early winter, huge pods of up to 50 individuals move into the field.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:09.320
to hunt. Herring from the North Atlantic come to these sheltered waters to overwinter in vast

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:28.210
shimmering clouds. Instead of using their speed to catch individual fish, as their salmon hunting

00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:29.970
cousins do in Johnston Strait,

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:34.860
The killers here use their skills as communicators and operate as a team.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:48.270
The herring are widely dispersed in quite deep water,

00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:52.990
and the killers concentrate them by swimming around the school in ever-tightening circles.

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:03.780
As they work, they signal to one another with high-pitched calls.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.810
They flash their white thanks, which seems to terrify the herring

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:11.370
and deters them from breaking through the cordon of encircling whales.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:23.980
Like wolves herding a mob of caravan, the whales concentrate the shoal into a tight ball

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:26.600
and drive it closer and closer to the surface.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.600
While some whales continue to keep the shoal herded together,

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:11.460
others move into it and stun the fish with powerful flicks of their huge tail.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:40.360
The three-tonne whales then start delicately to pick up the half-pound herrings one by one.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:45.160
An adult male will consume 400 or more in a day.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:59.980
The whale's killing technique is so effective that there are...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:07.420
There are plenty of dead herring left over for other creatures, such as Atlantic cod and the ever-present gulls.

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:19.000
Fish-eating whales, like these in Norway, hunt in large cooperative teams.

00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:23.280
But there are smaller groups elsewhere whose strategies are very different.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:29.620
They specialise in hunting warm-blooded prey, seals, dolphins and other whales.

00:20:35.820 --> 00:20:40.520
These other killer whales have spread to every corner of the seas in their search for food.

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:45.170
One of the most isolated communities of all has only recently been discovered

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:48.390
around a group of tiny islands in the southern Indian Ocean.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:57.410
South-east of the Cape of Good Hope, on the edge of the Antarctic, lie the Crozet Islands.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:04.640
It's one of the most lonely and inhospitable places in all the world.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.840
For much of the year, lashed by ferocious gales.

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:29.980
Every spring, particular groups of killers arrive here in Antioch.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.340
participation of a great feast to come.

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.170
A few islands in this vast expanse of ocean

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:41.170
that when the breeding season arrives,

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:44.790
the beaches become densely clouded by penguins and seals

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.530
seeking a place for their young.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.460
Human hunters once came here as well to slaughter the seals for oil.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:59.030
But now Christophe Guiné is one of only a few regular human visitors.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:06.210
He spent the past four seasons on the main island, Possession,

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:08.820
recording the daily activities of the whales.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:20.300
He lives for weeks on end in this small hut.

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:25.070
When I was a kid, I always had the dream to study killer whales.

00:22:25.480 --> 00:22:29.860
I was fascinated by these animals because they're powerful hunters.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.520
with a complex social life and highly intelligent.

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:39.880
I really wanted to understand all these wonderful creatures

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:44.590
live in a world that is so unknown to us.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.540
An underwater microphone out in the bay

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:51.620
transmits the sounds of the marine world back to his hut

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:55.830
so he can keep track of the 18 whale families who come to the island.

00:22:57.910 --> 00:22:59.670
One of these, a pod.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:10.470
is his main study group its male a1 was first identified in 1972 now he's in his 30s and with

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:18.940
him are two adult cows each with a calf the youngest adult female a3 is about 25 years old

00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:25.660
AFRI as a six-year-old female calf, Zoe.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:33.520
The other adult female is A2.

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:35.580
She's about 40 years old,

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:39.260
and she has a big nick at the base of her fin,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:48.240
and she has two smaller ones at the top of the fin.

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:54.050
A2 has a seven-year-old female calf, Mae.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:23:59.970
Each spring, A-pod...

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.600
visits Bay Ameriken.

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:04.330
It has a gently sloping sandy beach

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:06.810
and is protected from the prevailing westerlies.

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:10.480
So it's an ideal location for an elephant seal colony

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:12.840
and it's that which attracts the whales.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:18.080
The female seals come ashore to give birth in late September.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:23.260
Cures are always around such colonies,

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:26.380
snapping up anything edible, alive or dead.

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:35.410
They will even sip the rich milk seeping from a cow in labour.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.860
Most of the females give birth within two days of landing.

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:13.570
Immediately after birth, mother and infant become bonded by their smell, appearance, and particularly the unique sound of each other's bark.

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:29.700
The newborn seal must take on food as fast as possible, and fed by one of the richest of all milks.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.020
It will triple its weight in three weeks.

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:41.420
Every few days, Apod patrols the beach of Velimerekenne,

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:43.530
checking on the progress of the colony.

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:50.280
Very impressive to see the reaction of these male elephant seals

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:54.020
watching this dorsal thing and moving up as fast as they can.

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:56.920
They are really, really scared by killer whales.

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:59.940
And also, as soon as a female...

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.410
will sport a killer whale, then the other female will start to watch around and you can really feel

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:26.110
the fear expressed by these elephants here when they saw a dolphin. It's now three weeks into the

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:29.960
season and the first seal pups have been weaned. They're exhausted.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.400
Astrid mothers must now return to the sea to feed.

00:26:32.870 --> 00:26:37.790
But they're very weak, easy prey for the killer whales if they come within reach.

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:46.050
A female seal knows that the killers are out there,

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:49.130
and she stays within the protection of the shallow water,

00:26:49.370 --> 00:26:51.910
waiting for the best moment to run the blockade.

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:58.260
Another car's departure is complicated by her youngster's unwillingness to be abandoned.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.360
The pup wanted to go with its mother, and apparently she had to go.

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:08.200
And they were both moving along the shore and producing some noises,

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:15.480
which were detected by the whales, which both of them converged towards this female and this pup.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:19.220
But as they were a bit too high on the beach, she managed to escape them.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:26.540
The majority of the weaned pups have gathered in groups in the grass at the rear of the colony.

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:29.970
They will stay on shore for the next six weeks.

00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:35.060
during which time their puppy fat will be converted into a thick layer of insulating blubber.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:41.520
Most afternoons, they visit a stream which flows down through the colony to the bay.

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:57.130
Slowly, they acquire the swimming skills they will need in a few weeks' time

00:27:57.130 --> 00:27:59.970
in order to catch their prey, usually squirt.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.280
which they will chase in the dim depths of the Southern Ocean.

00:28:04.160 --> 00:28:21.440
But to do that, they have to get past the waiting killer whales.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:29.980
On the seaward side...

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.880
of the bar across the mouth of the creek, the young killer whales of Apod are training

00:28:34.880 --> 00:28:40.630
for their part in this annual drama. They're being taught the technique of snatching

00:28:40.630 --> 00:28:49.430
a pup from the shallows. Their mothers are showing them how this can be done by pushing

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:59.960
them ashore. Only the cows and calves of Apod take part in this beachy school.

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:04.520
the young ones learning through imitation, and the adults getting some practice.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:23.190
The male, A1, stays away from the beach.

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:27.350
His job will be to patrol the entrance to the bay in deeper water

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:29.970
and block off the escape of any seals that get...

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:42.990
past the beaching part. The technique they're practicing is a difficult and dangerous one.

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:47.650
They have to throw themselves right out of the water if they're to have a good chance

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:53.410
of grabbing a seal. The young calf, Zoe, has already got the basic idea.

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:12.190
When king penguins appear in the shallows, the other calf may co-opt them into the training program by using them as targets.

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:29.440
The weather is comparatively calm, but even so, beaching remains a risky business.

00:30:30.510 --> 00:30:34.670
A2 is obviously apprehensive, and as her calf May practices,

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:39.930
she positions herself so that if May gets stuck, she can push her back into deeper water.

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:49.640
A2 has good reason for being worried, for May did indeed get stuck a few years earlier.

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:51.350
Christophe saw it happen.

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:59.880
October 1988, we formed the calf May stranded in Bayamérica.

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:09.370
Maé was in a state of shock, lying motionless, calling to our mother, A2, who was patrolling along the shore.

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:17.840
We tried to roll Maé back into the water, but her 500 kilograms were too much for us.

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:29.700
We then decided to turn her head towards the sea, and each time a wave came, we pushed her a bit further, and rapidly...

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:36.320
May understood what was happening, and she managed to cover the last few meters by herself.

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:57.000
Years later, Christophe is back, and so is A2 and May.

00:31:58.230 --> 00:31:59.970
He had discovered that the whales are...

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.400
silent when hunting seals and he suspected that they were listening for the noises seals make

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:06.580
when entering the water.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:12.460
So when the whales were out in the bay,

00:32:12.796 --> 00:32:16.176
He imitated the splashing of a pup by stepping in the water.

00:32:17.916 --> 00:32:20.636
immediately a whale swam toward you.

00:32:24.284 --> 00:32:28.344
Very quickly, the whale realized that I was different from a seal pup.

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:38.100
I got a feeling that the whale was testing my reactions by opening its mouth, which is a threat.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:44.780
But on the other hand, on many occasions...

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:52.450
I get the impression that they are just curious towards me and want to interact and play with me.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:03.220
This inquisitiveness, this impulse to explore,

00:33:03.640 --> 00:33:07.220
is part of what makes them such successful and widespread hunters.

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:16.300
It's curiosity satisfied.

00:33:16.988 --> 00:33:18.048
the whale makes off.

00:33:27.548 --> 00:33:29.988
three kilometres east of the main elephant seal.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.760
colony, another protected beach provides 40,000 king penguins with a breeding site.

00:33:38.316 --> 00:33:41.496
They are another potential source of food for a pod.

00:33:57.068 --> 00:33:59.888
There's a small colony of elephant seal here as well.

00:34:07.804 --> 00:34:10.844
These brown chicks were hatched 10 months ago.

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:12.740
They will soon have to leave.

00:34:13.024 --> 00:34:14.404
and start feeding for themselves.

00:34:17.756 --> 00:34:21.496
Already, they're losing their down and sprouting their sea-going feathers.

00:34:32.684 --> 00:34:38.584
On each side of the king penguin colony lies a patch of water where the killer whales like to swim and rest.

00:34:39.084 --> 00:34:43.824
and this area is visited by APOD almost daily during the seal hunting season.

00:34:46.444 --> 00:34:48.244
It was first discovered in 1986.

00:34:48.876 --> 00:34:51.916
and Christoph spends many hours here observing the whales.

00:34:53.612 --> 00:34:57.692
This place is really unique, I mean, on Crozet, it's...

00:34:57.836 --> 00:34:59.836
the place where the world

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:02.180
will come and rest and relax.

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:06.090
And you will see them spending at this time of the year,

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:08.510
spending hours and hours just turning around.

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:11.170
And on this side of the bay,

00:35:11.290 --> 00:35:14.940
it will be mostly the females, A3 and A2.

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:16.880
which will spend most part of their time.

00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:18.380
And I mean, it's unique.

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:21.160
You can just see them running along the rocks

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:24.160
and spending quite a bit of time turning around.

00:35:32.236 --> 00:35:36.256
Even when the calves are several years old, they still keep close to their mothers.

00:35:38.092 --> 00:35:42.852
May idles along right under her mother's body, as she did when she was a nursing calf,

00:35:43.332 --> 00:35:45.392
reaffirming the close bond between them.

00:35:59.148 --> 00:35:59.828
A pod.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:05.170
spend as much as 20% of the day in this place to rest and play.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:08.860
It is a very important place for them,

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:11.920
part of the cultural tradition of the pod,

00:36:12.140 --> 00:36:15.510
which is used generation after generation.

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:19.050
And I'm pretty sure that in 20 years' time,

00:36:19.330 --> 00:36:22.790
the calves will still be using this area

00:36:22.790 --> 00:36:26.350
in the same way as their mothers do now.

00:36:27.950 --> 00:36:29.960
A big part of the attraction is the...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:36.120
kelp. Just as the whales of Vancouver Island enjoy a pebble massage, so these seem to love

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:38.480
having their backs stroked by the long fronds.

00:37:30.350 --> 00:37:50.440
Summer, the young elephant seals are beginning to gather along the shoreline,

00:37:50.730 --> 00:37:52.770
just near the killer whales rest area.

00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:59.980
Here, they have a good chance of picking up any pups that are incautious enough to stray.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:09.190
into deeper water. The pups will soon have to go to sea to feed but they will have to break through

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:18.770
the killer's blockade just as their mothers did six weeks earlier. Meanwhile for the whales there's

00:38:18.770 --> 00:38:28.140
an endless supply of smaller prey, king penguins from the nearby colony. Adults going for a wash

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:29.980
are joined by the newly fledged young.

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:34.120
now in their sea-going plumage and taking to the water for the first time.

00:38:41.130 --> 00:38:44.050
Those adults with chicks that still need looking after

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:46.290
are setting off for their feeding grounds,

00:38:46.430 --> 00:38:50.150
which lie 600 kilometres farther south towards the Antarctic.

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:28.960
busy corridors present the whales with an endless possession of potential food.

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:42.150
Suddenly, a pod shows itself, and the adult penguins scatter in panic.

00:39:52.480 --> 00:39:57.480
But the youngsters, getting their first sea experience, don't realise what danger they're in.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:17.870
Some shelter among the kelp fronds.

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:51.750
...penguin to practice her hunting skills.

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:54.160
It's a game of life and death.

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:57.990
Death inevitably for a penguin, but life for May.

00:40:58.430 --> 00:40:59.970
For unless she becomes an expert...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:12.420
hunter very quickly, she will not survive long. The giant petrels, the vultures of the ocean,

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:17.450
know there are likely to be tasty pickings when killer whales are about, and they follow them

00:41:17.450 --> 00:41:29.550
around in anticipation of a few scraps. Having played with the penguin,

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:33.600
like a cat with a mouse, May abandons it to the petrels.

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:11.780
Every ten days or so, great gales sweep into the bay,

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:14.080
whipping up waves ten feet high.

00:42:14.600 --> 00:42:18.060
But such conditions don't hamper the activities of the killer whales.

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:34.950
May takes advantage of the surf to get an extra turn of speed

00:42:34.950 --> 00:42:38.490
as she chases some adult penguins returning from a feeding trip.

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:14.480
The hapless loser becomes a toy as the winner tosses it into the air with a clout of her great tail.

00:43:27.290 --> 00:43:29.980
After the gale passes, cold air from the Antal...

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.300
Arctic races in from the southwest, bringing squalls and flurries of snow.

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:43.540
The penguins, both adult and chicks, huddle together in groups, giving one another what

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:45.420
protection they can from the bitter wind.

00:44:06.520 --> 00:44:10.520
The elephant seals sit out the storm, protected by their thick layer of blubber.

00:44:11.370 --> 00:44:16.290
And Christoph continues his observations, recording the whale's behaviour, whatever the conditions.

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:29.960
The next depression brings torrential...

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:47.630
rain and heavy runoff floods the river. The current in the swollen river is now so strong

00:44:47.630 --> 00:44:53.450
that some of the inexperienced young seals are swept downstream before they realise what is happening.

00:45:13.470 --> 00:45:14.970
The whales know well enough.

00:45:15.590 --> 00:45:20.130
Years of experience have taught them that heavy rains at this time of the year will bring a feast.

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:23.000
And they gather around the river mouth and collect it.

00:45:27.040 --> 00:45:28.820
The seal pups do have some protection.

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:29.980
The flooded river...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.690
waters are laden with sediment. That and the turbulence make it difficult for the whales

00:45:34.690 --> 00:45:49.300
to see or hear the pups, and some do escape. But with their reserves of fat running low,

00:45:49.780 --> 00:45:52.380
the young seals have to try and reach the open sea.

00:46:03.610 --> 00:46:08.510
The high water in the wake of the storm allows the killer whales to get close enough inshore

00:46:08.510 --> 00:46:12.270
to use the beaching technique they've been practicing for the last few weeks.

00:46:14.180 --> 00:46:16.820
The females of A-pod patrol the shallows.

00:46:17.500 --> 00:46:21.920
Every now and then, one of them surfaces briefly to check on the position of the seals.

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:25.340
Then comes the attack.

00:46:27.840 --> 00:46:29.980
A-3 hurls herself up the beach to...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:41.440
grab a pup. She only just gets a grip on it. This is a very dangerous place for her, and she has to

00:46:41.440 --> 00:46:52.060
retreat. But she manages to drag the pup with her. A pod's success rate when hunting in this way is

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:58.200
very low. Only one pup for every 20 hours of petroleum. But elsewhere in the world, other

00:46:58.200 --> 00:46:59.820
families using similar methods.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:14.580
are more successful. 8,000 kilometres to the west of the Crozes, another population of killer whales

00:47:14.580 --> 00:47:24.400
has discovered an even more favourable place for beaching. Here on the coast of Argentina,

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:29.660
there are many bays with pebbled shores that are much steeper than the shelving beach of Bay American.

00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:32.340
crozes, so there's much less chance

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:34.040
of a beaching whale getting stranded.

00:47:38.150 --> 00:47:40.270
The daily drama starts in Medina

00:47:40.270 --> 00:47:42.060
Bay at low tide with a

00:47:42.060 --> 00:47:48.130
practice session. Whales have been studied

00:47:48.130 --> 00:47:50.170
for several years, and they too have

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:57.760
been given names. Blanche

00:47:57.760 --> 00:47:59.860
is an adult female, and the most

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:13.900
experienced beaching, and Serge, the young male with her, is also very accomplished. The steepness

00:48:13.900 --> 00:48:18.920
of the beach allows them to arch their bodies so they can pivot on their belly and slide back on

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:19.560
the next wave.

00:48:30.636 --> 00:48:33.456
Several kilometres along the coast from the practice beach

00:48:33.456 --> 00:48:36.416
is the first of five sea lion colonies.

00:48:40.364 --> 00:48:41.204
At low time.

00:48:41.516 --> 00:48:42.716
An exposed reef.

00:48:42.896 --> 00:48:48.936
provides good protection for the sealions and the pups can play safely in the pools left by the outgoing water.

00:48:51.884 --> 00:48:53.404
But the title range is enormous.

00:48:53.804 --> 00:48:55.724
And when the water begins to flow back,

00:48:55.948 --> 00:48:58.268
The protecting reefs are soon submerged.

00:49:02.652 --> 00:49:05.572
Killer whales know that now they can get across the reef.

00:49:06.204 --> 00:49:10.864
First to make the dash are two brothers who have been hunting here for 17 years.

00:49:11.516 --> 00:49:16.696
That distinctive bent fin belongs to the younger of the two, Big Mel, who is 20.

00:49:20.092 --> 00:49:21.192
That's Brother Bernard.

00:49:21.436 --> 00:49:22.856
older by 10 years.

00:49:24.636 --> 00:49:29.616
As the whales angle in towards the beach, they listen for the splashing of the unsuspecting pups.

00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:35.960
Having spotted their quarry, the brothers seem to employ a decoy strategy.

00:49:36.530 --> 00:49:38.670
Mel sails past in full view.

00:49:47.940 --> 00:49:53.210
Behind him comes Bernard, angling his tall fin downwards to remain inconspicuous.

00:49:55.840 --> 00:49:59.970
Bernard remains silent except for an occasional sonar click and a...

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.500
like signal which sometimes precedes an attack.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:33.640
A youngster further along the beach is oblivious of the danger.

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:45.980
a successful attack.

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:47.560
needs precise judgment.

00:50:51.148 --> 00:50:52.488
and even Big Mel.

00:50:52.844 --> 00:50:53.564
often misses.

00:50:58.284 --> 00:50:59.964
Bernard's bow wave of clear

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.260
water acts like a lens through which you can see his target.

00:51:13.820 --> 00:51:16.940
Mel and Bernard's immense bodies need a lot of feeding.

00:51:17.276 --> 00:51:20.336
Each has to eat three pups a day to keep up his energy.

00:51:28.508 --> 00:51:29.868
As high tide approaches,

00:51:30.124 --> 00:51:34.544
the whales move several kilometres along the coast to the last of the colonies.

00:51:40.236 --> 00:51:41.896
reefs running out from the shore

00:51:42.156 --> 00:51:44.116
form a channel 100 metres wide.

00:51:44.972 --> 00:51:47.232
The adult sea lions have to get across it.

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:49.920
if they are to reach their feeding grounds out at sea.

00:51:52.524 --> 00:51:55.084
Pups often follow their parents across this channel,

00:51:55.084 --> 00:51:59.624
and it's here at High Water that Serge and Blanche get their best hunting.

00:52:05.370 --> 00:52:12.030
As they swim in over the top of the reef, they drop down into the deeper water of the channel, an ideal place for an ambush.

00:52:17.870 --> 00:52:24.010
Like their cousins in the crozes, the whales use their sharp sense of hearing to detect the swimming noise of the sea lions.

00:52:37.900 --> 00:52:43.540
Remarkably, Blanche can tell the difference between adult and baby sea lions by the splashing sound they make,

00:52:43.760 --> 00:52:47.430
even in rough water like this, and she closes in for the kill.

00:53:04.220 --> 00:53:06.580
The two brothers also have a game plan.

00:53:07.540 --> 00:53:16.540
He is the better hunter and usually leads the attack,

00:53:16.760 --> 00:53:20.040
while Bernard stays behind to pick up any confused pups

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:22.320
that, in their panic, try to swim out to sea.

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:28.130
This time, Bernard is lucky.

00:53:52.110 --> 00:53:55.290
As the successful hunters toy with their still-living victim,

00:53:56.060 --> 00:53:59.600
clouting it with their tails like the Norwegian killers stunning the herring,

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:24.000
underwater world rings with their loud, excited calls. This cavorting with their prize may

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:29.930
look like a display of wild triumph, but it is certainly also a way for the younger members

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:34.820
the team to practice the skills needed for the chases that later in the year they will have to

00:54:34.820 --> 00:54:57.340
make in the open sea. And then when young and old have shared the catch and eaten their fill

00:54:57.340 --> 00:54:59.800
there comes a most unexpected incident.

00:55:02.240 --> 00:55:19.600
The mosque is not killed, but carried gently back to the shore and set free.

00:55:19.840 --> 00:55:29.980
Is this an act of mercy, or is it just...

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:41.110
heart of play behaviour. Who can say? How can one enter the mind of a killer whale? That these whales

00:55:41.110 --> 00:55:46.270
are highly intelligent is clear from the fact that each community around the world has developed

00:55:46.270 --> 00:55:53.220
its own vocabulary of sounds and its own skills and traditions. As we watch them, we might think

00:55:53.220 --> 00:55:57.720
that we can detect signs of anger, pleasure and curiosity in their behaviour.

00:56:00.120 --> 00:56:04.980
But notions of morality, of good and evil, we should not attribute to them.

00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:08.300
Those are the product of the human mind.

00:56:09.210 --> 00:56:12.470
We shouldn't regard the killers as either cruel or merciful.

00:56:13.310 --> 00:56:18.300
They are what they are, magnificent animals that must kill to live.

00:57:00.350 --> 00:57:00.510
Thank you.
